 concepts, national restaurant chains, hospitals, nursing homes, hotels and motels, country clubs, government and military organizations, colleges and universities, and retail locations. The company was formerly known as USF Holding Corp. and changed its name to US Foods Holding Corp. in February 2016. US Foods Holding Corp. was incorporated in 2007 and is headquartered in Rosemont, Illinois.","ceo_bio":"Mr. David E. Flitman | CEO & Chairman of the Board | Age 61 | Compensation: $4.8M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 30K people. Revenue per employee of $1.3M ranks in the top quartile among Consumer Defensive peers, improving at 6%/yr \u2014 growing workforce leverage. Each employee generates $32K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $3K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 4\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags low compensation governance risk (2/10).","financial_summary":["US Foods Holding Corp. is a large-cap consumer defensive business with a $23.1B market cap on $39.4B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +5% over the past three years, which puts it in mature, slow-growth territory.","Returns on capital run at 8% against a 9% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests, and 17% gross margins are typical of commodity economics where competitors can replicate the product cheaply.","The business throws off $959M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 2 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 2.03\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, debt is moderate at 3.1\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, serviceable in normal conditions but worth watching through a downturn, and interest coverage runs at 3.9\u00d7.","Shareholder yield of 4.0% reflects a genuine commitment to returning capital rather than empire-building, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome, and profit capture is only 0.4\u00d7 the fair share, suggesting no structural edge inside its industry."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Price sits 42% above estimated fair value \u2014 no margin of safety left, and the cushion against estimate error has been bid away (confirmed by 3 other valuation measures)","sev":"red","cat":"valuation","magnitude":0.42400223576640783},{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Fragmented industry with below-average profit capture \u2014 no moat to protect margins","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Above-average short interest at 7.3% of float","sev":"amber","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Insiders are net sellers (100% sell ratio, net $5.5M) \u2014 the people closest to the business are reducing their personal stake","sev":"amber","cat":"insider"},{"text":"Gross margin trails most Consumer Defensive peers \u2014 competitors appear to have the pricing hand","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Significant goodwill (42% of assets) from past acquisitions \u2014 a soft asset a conservative appraisal discounts to zero","sev":"amber","cat":"balance_sheet"},{"text":"Above-average market volatility (VIX 26)","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"GLP-1 and health-trend demand destruction \u2014 weight-loss drugs and wellness shifts are structurally reducing consumption in snack, beverage, and packaged food categories.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Piotroski score 7/9 \u2014 financials improving across profitability, leverage and efficiency","Altman Z comfortably in the safe zone \u2014 a fortress balance sheet on this measure","Consumer Defensive sector showing strong relativ